== 1. The Most Eminent Saints in Private Prayer

First, the most eminent saints, both in the Old and New Testament, have applied themselves to private prayer. Moses was alone on the mountain with God forty days and forty nights (Exod 34:28). So Abraham fills his mouth with arguments and reasons the case out alone with God in prayer to prevent Sodom’s desolation and destruction, and never leaves off pleading and praying until he has brought God down from fifty to ten (Gen 18:22–32). And in Genesis 21:33 you have Abraham again at his private prayers: “And Abraham planted a grove in Beersheba, and there he called upon the name of the #smallcaps[Lord], the Eternal God.” Why did Abraham plant a grove, except to have a most private place in which to pray and pour out his soul before the Lord? So Isaac: “Early in the evening, Isaac went out to the field to meditate” (Gen 24:63). The Hebrew word #emph[lasuach] that is here rendered “meditate” signifies to pray as well as to meditate, and it is often used so. It is a comprehensive word that takes in both prayer and meditation. So you will find Jacob at his private prayer: “So Jacob was left all alone, and there a man wrestled with him until daybreak” (Gen 32:24–28). When Jacob was all alone in the dark night, and when his joints were out of joint, he so wrestles and weeps, and weeps and wrestles, in private prayer that at last, as a prince, he prevails with God (Hos 12:3–4). So David: “But I call to God, and the #smallcaps[Lord] saves me. Evening, morning, and noon I pray and cry aloud, and He hears my voice” (Ps 55:16–17).

So Daniel was in private prayer three times a day: “Now when Daniel learned that the document had been signed, he went into his house, where the windows of his upper room opened toward Jerusalem, and three times a day he got down on his knees, prayed, and gave thanks to his God, just as he had done before” (Dan 6:10). Daniel had accustomed himself to private prayer. He went to his closet before he went to his public employment and affairs of state; and when he came back for his midday meal, he turned first into his room to serve his God and refresh his soul before he sat down to feast his body. And at the end of the day, when he had dispatched his business with men, he made it his business to wait on God in his room. So Jonah keeps up private prayer when he was “inside the fish,” yes, when he was in “the belly of hell” (Jonah 2:1–2). So we have Elijah at prayer under the juniper tree (1 Kgs 19:4). So Hannah: “Hannah was praying in her heart, and though her lips were moving, her voice could not be heard” (1 Sam 1:13). The very soul of prayer lies in pouring out the soul before God, as Hannah did (v. 15). Nor was Rebekah a stranger to this duty, who, when the baby struggled in her womb, “went to inquire of the #smallcaps[Lord]” (Gen 25:22); that is, she went to some secret place to pray, say Calvin, Musculus, Mercerus, and others. So Saul is no sooner converted than he at once turns to private prayer: “Get up!” the Lord told him. “Go to the house of Judas on Straight Street and ask for a man from Tarsus named Saul, for he is praying” (Acts 9:11). Though he was a strict Pharisee, yet he had never prayed to good effect before, nor ever prayed in private before. The Pharisees used to pray on the corners of the streets, and not in the corners of their houses. And after his conversion he was frequently in private prayer, as you may see by comparing these scriptures together: Rom 1:9; Eph 1:15–16; Phil 1:3–4; 2 Tim 1:3. So Epaphras was a warm man in closet prayer (Col 4:12–13); so Cornelius had devoted himself to private prayer (Acts 10:2, 4); and so Peter goes up to the housetop to pray: “The next day at about the sixth hour, as the men were approaching the city on their journey, Peter went up on the roof to pray” (Acts 10:9). Peter went up onto the roof not only to avoid distraction, but so that he might be all the more secret in his private devotion.

Eusebius tells us of James, called Justus, that his knees had grown hard and calloused from kneeling so much in private prayer. And Nazianzen reports of his sister Gorgonia that her knees seemed to cleave to the earth from her often praying in private. And Gregory says of his aunt Trucilla that her elbows were as hard as horn from often leaning on her desk at private prayer. I have read of a devout person who, when the set time for his private devotion had come, whatever company he was in, would break away from them with this neat and graceful excuse: “I have a friend waiting for me; farewell.” And there was once a great lady of this land who would frequently withdraw from the company of lords and ladies of high rank who came to visit her, rather than lose her set times of waiting on God in her closet. She would, as they called it, rudely take her leave of them, so that she might attend the Lord of lords in private. She would spare what time she could to show her favors, civilities, and courtesies among her relatives and friends; but she would never allow them to rob God of His time, nor her soul of the comfort and communion which she used to enjoy when she was with God in her closet. And indeed, one hour’s communion with God in one’s closet is to be preferred to the greatest and best company in the world.

There was a child of a Christian gentlewoman who was so given to prayer from his infancy that before he could speak well, he used to get off alone and go to prayer. As he grew, he was more frequent in prayer and in withdrawing himself from company. He would ask his mother very strange questions, far above the capacity of one of his years. But at last, when this child was only five years old and was whipping his top, he suddenly flung away his whip and top, ran to his mother, and with great joy said to her, “Mother, I must go to God; will you go with me?” She answered, “My dear child, how do you know you will go to God?” He answered, “God has told me so, for I love God, and God loves me.” She answered, “Dear child, I must go when God pleases. But why will you not stay with me?” The child answered, “I will not stay; I must go to God.” And the child did not live more than a month after, and never cared for play again; but falling sick, he would always be saying that he must go to God, he must go to God. Thus sometimes “from the mouths of children and infants” God has “ordained praise” (Matt 21:16). Certainly such persons will be ripe for heaven early who begin early to seek God in a closet, in a corner.

Eusebius reports of the emperor Constantine that every day he used to shut himself up in some secret place in his palace, and there, on bended knees, made his devout prayers and soliloquies to God. “My God and I are good company,” said the famous Dr. Sibbes. A man whose soul holds fellowship with God in a closet, in a hole, behind the door, or in a desert, a den, a dungeon, will find more real pleasure, more choice delight, and more full contentment there than in the palace of a prince.

By all these famous instances, you see that the people of God in all ages have devoted themselves to private prayer. O friends, these pious examples should be very awakening, very convicting, and very encouraging to you#super[+]. Certainly it is as much your#super[+] duty as it is your glory to follow these pious patterns that are now set before you. As these scriptures witness: “So you will follow in the ways of the good, and keep to the paths of the righteous.… You#super[+] are to imitate me, just as I imitate Christ.… Join one another in following my example, brothers, and carefully observe those who walk according to the pattern we set for you#super[+].… Whatever you#super[+] have learned or received or heard from me, or seen in me, put it into practice. And the God of peace will be with you#super[+].… And you#super[+] became imitators of us and of the Lord when you welcomed the message … in spite of your great suffering.… Then you#super[+] will not be sluggish, but will imitate those who through faith and patience inherit what has been promised” (Prov 2:20; 1 Cor 11:1; Phil 3:17; 4:9; 1 Thess 1:6; Heb 6:12). See also 2 Tim 3:10–12, 14; Titus 2:7. It was an excellent law that the Ephesians made, that men should set the best patterns before themselves and always bear some eminent man in mind. Bad men are wonderfully in love with bad examples (Jer 44:16–17). The Indian, hearing that his ancestors had gone to hell, said that then he would go there too. Some men have a mind to go to hell for company’s sake. Oh, that we were as much in love with the examples of good men as others are in love with the examples of bad men! Then we would be in our closets more often than we are now. Oh, that our eyes were more fixed on the pious examples of all who have in them #emph[aliquid Christi] (anything of Christ), as Bucer said! Shall we love to look on the pictures of our friends, and shall we not love to look on the pious examples of those who are the living and lovely picture of Christ? The pious examples of others should be the mirrors by which we dress ourselves. He is the best and wisest Christian who copies the fairest pattern in Scripture, who imitates the Christians who are most eminent in grace, and who have been most exercised in closet prayer and in the most secret duties of religion.

Jerome, having read the life and death of Hilarion, one who lived most Christianly and died most comfortably, folded up the book, saying, “Well, Hilarion shall be the champion that I will follow. His good life shall be my example, and his godly death my precedent.” It is a noble thing to live and die by the examples of the most eminent saints.
